1	# Template Method
2	
3	Write the fixed sequence of steps once, in a base class, and let each case fill in only the steps that differ.
4	
5	## Use it when
6	
7	- several cases follow the same sequence of steps, and only some steps change from case to case;
8	- a change to the sequence itself (a new first step, a new check at the end) should apply to every case at once;
9	- new cases following the same sequence are expected.
10	
11	## Do not use it when
12	
13	- there is no shared sequence: each case is a different algorithm (that is Strategy);
14	- there are two cases and the shared part is a couple of lines;
15	- the only shared thing is a formatting helper: a utility function is enough, and it is not a Template Method.
16	
17	## Shape
18	
19	```java
20	abstract class BoardGame {
21	    // the template method: the sequence lives here and nowhere else
22	    public final Result play(List<Player> players) {
23	        Board board = setUp(players);
24	        while (!isOver(board)) {
25	            playTurn(board, nextPlayer(board, players));
26	        }
27	        return decideWinner(board);
28	    }
29	
30	    protected abstract Board setUp(List<Player> players);
31	    protected abstract boolean isOver(Board board);
32	    protected abstract void playTurn(Board board, Player player);
33	    protected abstract Result decideWinner(Board board);
34	
35	    protected Player nextPlayer(Board board, List<Player> players) { /* default rotation; a hook */ }
36	}
37	
38	final class Checkers extends BoardGame { /* only the four steps */ }
39	final class Reversi extends BoardGame { /* only the four steps */ }
40	```
41	
42	The same idea works with composition: one generic runner that receives the varying steps as objects or functions
43	(`new GameRunner(setUp, isOver, playTurn, decideWinner)`), as long as every case goes through that runner.
44	
45	## Done right when
46	
47	- the sequence is written once, in the template method (marked `final` when the language allows);
48	- each case implements only the steps that vary, and none of them repeats the sequence;
49	- every case uses the skeleton, including the cases that existed before it was introduced;
50	- a change to the sequence is one edit, in the template method.
51	
52	## Usual mistakes
53	
54	- A common interface with one method (`run()`), where each implementation still writes the whole sequence: the
55	  cases look uniform from outside, but the skeleton is copied inside each one.
56	- A shared helper that formats the output, while each case still calls the steps in order by itself.
57	- The new cases use the skeleton and the old ones stay as they were.
58	- Steps so coarse that one "step" contains almost the whole sequence.
59	
